//#region Archetypes
// archetypes help define individual people and how they're constructed and should behave, without replicating a ton of data on all of them

// description: user-readable short description
// wealth: wealth rating, determines disposable cash (0-100 struggling, 2-300 poor, 3-400 decent, 4-500 middling, 5-600 well-to-do, 7-800, wealthy, 8-900 rich, 900-1000 mega-rich)
// age: age in years
// height: height rating (0-1000 with 500 being average human height)
// plumpness: plumpness mass rating (0-1000)
// muscle: muscle mass rating (0-1000)
// breast size: breast size rating (0-1000)
// areola size: areola size rating (0-1000)
// penis size: penis size rating (0-1000)
// penis girth: penis girth rating (0-1000)
// ass size: ass size rating (0-1000)
// style: from the Style database
// perfected: maximum skill levels (not unusual for this to be empty)
// proficient: high skill levels
// skilled: moderate skill levels
// dabbling: low (but non-zero) skill levels (any skills not in the above four lists will be zero, so all main skills should probably be in one of the lists)
// inclinations: inclinations people of this archetype have, which will heavily affect their behavior
// subarchetypes: further subdivides archetypes into subsets with different skills/inclinations
// additional inclinations: some overlap with subarchetypes, but basically a way to possibly add other individual inclinations
// friendliness mod: modifier for charisma checks determining if this persons likes you. it's a difficulty mod, so higher = worse

// yeah, some of this stuff is kinda reductive and filled with things that shouldn't really correlate, don't worry about it
// no judgments here, but things have to be kinda constrained or everything starts looking a bit madlibs
// we want that handcrafted and not randomly-generated look even though it is partly randomly generated
// and we also want to mostly avoid extremes (leave those for the player to pursue, or as characteristics of unique NPCs)
// and yeah some get some kinda wild inclinations, but that's how we get them to behave distinctly
// we are making characters, not trying to accurately simulate the entire realm of possibility

